{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And Elohim made the raqia</b>. This verse had no need to be stated, since the previous verse already said ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים״ etc., in which it should have completed by saying ״וַיְהִי כֵן״. Furthermore, it appears to me that it would not be proper to say ״וַיְהִי כֵן״ after saying ״וַיַּעַשׂ אֱלֹהִים״; rather, after the statement ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים״ He should have informed us that He spoke and it came to be." "<b>And it appears</b> to say that three actions were performed on the raqia. The first is the creation of its substratum in actuality, and this is what was created on the first day by the first utterance:" "<b>And the second</b> — that HaShem said to it that it should make a raqia, meaning that it should be stretched out and made into a curtain, and HaShem measured for it the measure of the stretching, which is enough to separate between water and water, and this is what is stated in the previous verse (Genesis 1:6)." "<b>And the third</b> — because the heavens are spherical and convex, for we cannot say that the shape of this raqia differs from all the [other] heavens, and in the utterance that He said ״יְהִי רָקִיעַ״ He said nothing except that it should be stretched out; but the arrangement of its form — whether it would be stretched like a curtain evenly, or convex at its edges, or inclined to one of the four directions of the world — this is not conveyed in the statement of HaShem saying ״יְהִי רָקִיעַ״. Therefore He returned and said <b>וַיַּעַשׂ</b>, meaning He fixed the arrangement of its placement according to the order of the formations of the heavens, which are like an inverted bowl facing downward with its back upward, for reasons known to the Creator of the universe. And He returned to say <b>וַיַּבְדֵּל</b> etc., because the order of the making that the Creator prepared is the contrary of the adjacent statement that He would separate between etc., for in this order there is no vessel preventing [the mingling of] these two bodies of water. Therefore He said that even though He made it in this order, nevertheless ״וַיַּבְדֵּל בֵּין״ etc. <b>And concerning</b> these two particulars the verse states <b>וַיַּעַשׂ</b> — that He fixed it in the order that was upright in His eyes, and that He brought about in it the possibility that it could endure even in this order." "<b>And He was precise</b> to say <b>אֲשֶׁר מֵעַל</b> etc., to say that even though it is made in this order, He separated between the waters that are above it — something whose existence is impossible in the world. And after all this He said <b>וַיְהִי כֵן</b>, going back to what is written in the first and second verses, for when He said to it to become stretched out, so it was; and when He said to it to become a separator, even though its arrangement is not according to nature to separate in this order as mentioned, וַיְהִי כֵן. Or it is possible that the reason He delayed saying <b>וַיְהִי כֵן</b> until after the verse ״וַיַּעַשׂ״ is that He did not wish to say וַיְהִי כֵן until He completed the making with all the conditions of creation." "<b>And after</b> I wrote this, I found that a full verse of Scripture testifies to our interpretation, as it is written (Isaiah 42:5): ״כֹּה אָמַר [etc.] בּוֹרֵא הַשָּׁמַיִם וְנוֹטֵיהֶם״ — see that it mentions two things: the creation alone and the stretching alone, and this is what I explained concerning the acts of the first and second [days]: for on the first day there was the creation, and on the second day there was the stretching; and included in the stretching and its manner are the two things to which we alluded." "<b>And I saw</b> the saying of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, in Bereshit Rabbah (chapter 4): ״וַיַּעַשׂ אֱלֹהִים אֶת הָרָקִיעַ״ — this is one of the verses with which ben Zoma shook the world, and is it not [written] in a single utterance ״בִּדְבַר ה׳ שָׁמַיִם נַעֲשׂוּ״ (Psalms 33:6) — end quote. And according to our approach, the earth is quiet from its commotion; and perhaps what shook the world for ben Zoma was before the reconciliation of the verses, but after the reconciliation of the verses the earth rested quiet." "<b>Or</b> it may say along this line, according to what I explained in the verse ״וַיֹּאמֶר אֱלֹהִים יִקָּווּ״ etc., that there are two kinds of waters — one male and one female. Accordingly, since all the waters were intermixed in the manner that light and darkness were intermixed, a little here a little there, as they said of blessed memory (Bereshit Rabbah 3) וְהִבְדִּיל ה׳ בֵּין הָאוֹר וְגוֹ׳ — likewise the supernal waters, meaning the elevated ones, which are the male waters, were intermixed with the lower waters in degree. And now HaShem said that the firmament would make two separations: the first, that it would refine one from the other according to what the Holy One, blessed be He, had imprinted in it — that it would refine one from the other; and the second, that they would remain separated perpetually through its intermediary, as it would interpose between the two of them. And accordingly, when He said <b>יְהִי רָקִיעַ בְּתוֹךְ הַמָּיִם</b>, here He intended the act of refining that separates between the two waters — which is why He said <b>בְּתוֹךְ הַמָּיִם</b>, because all the waters were intermixed. And afterwards HaShem prepared the firmament so that it would be ready to interpose between them perpetually, and this is the second separation; and concerning both of them the verse says ״וְיְהִי כֵן״."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T18:54:07.712042413Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}